@startuml
' Software architecture: layers of the system - Inventory & POS System v0.20.5 (report, Lecture 6; drawn 2026-10-14)
' Edit: paste into draw.io (Arrange > Insert > Advanced > PlantUML) or planttext.com
skinparam defaultFontName Arial
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor #333333
skinparam rectangle {
  BackgroundColor White
  BorderColor Black
}
skinparam usecase {
  BackgroundColor White
  BorderColor Black
}
skinparam class {
  BackgroundColor White
  BorderColor Black
  HeaderBackgroundColor #E6F1EE
}
skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor White
  BorderColor Black
}
skinparam state {
  BackgroundColor White
  BorderColor Black
}
skinparam sequence {
  ParticipantBackgroundColor White
  ParticipantBorderColor Black
  LifeLineBorderColor #888888
  ActorBorderColor Black
  ActorBackgroundColor White
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFFBEA
  BorderColor #C9B458
}
skinparam swimlaneWidth same
hide circle

skinparam rectangle {
  BackgroundColor White
  BorderColor Black
}
rectangle "<b>1. User interface</b>\nHTML pages (Jinja2 templates), theme.css (light / dark), Bootstrap 5.3.3,\nBootstrap Icons, Inter font, small scripts (change preview, quick cash, Saving...)" as ui
rectangle "<b>2. User communication and security</b>\nFlask routes and forms, Flask-Login (login records, 8-hour idle limit), admin_required (403),\nCSRF token check, login attempt limit, security headers, file upload checks" as sec
rectangle "<b>3. Application logic</b>\nBasket and sale ticket, stock and recipe checks (Recipes, can_make), payments (cash, GCash),\natomic stock updates, ingredient history, low-stock rules, sales report, quick cash" as logic
rectangle "<b>4. Data access</b>\nSQLAlchemy models (models.py), upgrade_database() adds new columns and indexes" as data
rectangle "<b>5. Storage</b>\nSQLite file instance/inventory.db, secret key file, pictures in static/products and static/avatars" as store
ui -down-> sec
sec -down-> logic
logic -down-> data
data -down-> store
note right of sec
  Each layer only uses
  the layer below it.
end note
@enduml
